        {
          "id": 3496045956,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "Plus, it's `_USE_32BIT_TIME_T`, and it forces `time_t` to be *32* bits; see, for example, [Microsoft's C-library Visual Studio 2015 man page for `time()`, `_time32()`, and `_time64()`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/c-runtime-library/reference/time-time32-time64?view=msvc-140) - it's been that way for 11 years.\n\nI.e., *even on 32-bit Windows builds*, I think we get a 64-bit `time_t` by default.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
